    "lawCode": "Add Art 9-C §342, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2997\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to the installation\nor use of solar power systems within a homeowners' association\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow the installation or use of\nsolar power systems (SBS) within homeowner associations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. The Real Property Law is amended by adding a new ARTICLE 9-C\nSOLAR RIGHTS ACT § 342. Certain covenants, conditions, and restrictions\nof homeowners' association prohibited. 1. Defines \"restriction on use\"\nand \"solar power system\". 2. A homeowners' association my not adopt or\nenforce any rules or regulations that would effectively prohibit, or\nimpose unreasonable limitations on, the installation or use of a solar\npower system. A restriction on use which effectively prohibits the\ninstallation or use of a solar power system is unenforceable and shall\nbe void as contrary to public policy. For the purpose of this subdivi-\nsion, an unreasonable limitation includes, but is not limited to, any\nrestriction on use that: (a) inhibits the SPS from functioning at its\nintended maximum efficiency; or (b) increases the SPS's installation or\nmaintenance costs by more than 10% of the total cost of the initial\ninstallation of the system. 3. Notwithstanding subdivision two of this\nsection, a homeowners' association my adopt or enforce a restriction on\nuse to prohibit the installation of a SPS that is located on property\nowned by the homeowners' association or that is located on property\nowned in common by the members of the homeowners association. 4. Any\ndenial of a homeowner's installation of a SPS by an association must\ninclude details for the rejection. 5. A SPS must meet local, state or\nfederal law, rule or regulation on health and safety standards and those\nrequirements imposed by state and local permitting authorities. 6. A\nperson may bring a civil action in a court of competent jurisdiction for\nany violation of this section. In any action brought pursuant to this\nsection, the court may award the appropriate remedies and costs of liti-\ngation to the prevailing party.\n\nSection 2. Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\n6 was removed from Section 1 which state, a person may bring a civil\naction in a court of competent jurisdiction for any violation of this\nsection. In any action brought pursuant to this section, the court may\naward the appropriate remedies and costs of litigation to the prevailing\nparty.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe New York State Governor and Legislature have embarked upon a multi-\nyear, multi-pronged initiative to promote solar investment and use. To\nmeet the goals, however, solar power opportunities must be expanded to\nthose, who for a variety of reasons, have been prevented from installing\nsolar systems, particularly solar arrays, on their homes. Most homeowner\nassociations, for example, have not adapted their by-lays to provide\nhomeowners with the option. States such as Arizona, California, Florida\nand Massachusetts have remedied the problem by enacting laws to prohibit\nhomeowner associations from banning or curtailing the installation of\nsolar arrays on one's roof.  Compounding the problem is the lack of\nconsistency regarding homeowner association by-laws. There is no common\nstandard governing the installation and most if not all homeowner asso-\nciations reject such installation without citing reasons. Frequently,\nlitigation results with dissimilar outcomes. This legislation would\npermit homeowners the right to install solar arrays in consultation with\ntheir homeowners' association. It would thus expand the use of solar\npower systems for homeowners, while granting a role in the decision-mak-\ning process to the homeowner associations.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA. 5896-A and S. 4742-A of 2019/2020\nA. 3691 and S. 7735 of 2017/2018\nA. 6878-C and S. 7679-B of 2015/2016\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no fiscal implications associated with this legislation.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
